In article <1990May10.093959.1473@gdr.bath.ac.uk> exspes@gdr.bath.ac.uk (P E Smee) writes:
>Actually, the present handling of memorized spells is one of the few
>things about NetHack that REALLY annoys me.  It feels counter-
>realistic, and certainly against the spirit of all other games I've
>played.  Why?  Well, seems to me that using a spell should help you
>engrave it in your memory, and also increase the probability that it
>will work effectively in the future.  (Practice makes perfect. :-)
>
>My feeling is that you should be able to remember a spell (without
>using it) only for a limited period (500 turns? 1000 turns?) after
>reading the spellbook; and that a use should 'refresh' your memory and
>restart the timer.  In other words, NOT practicing them would lead you
>to forget them; practicing them would help you remember them.

There are 2 different issues here: reliability and reusability. Reliability
of spells (e.g. chance of spell failure) is done in a variety of ways within
the realm of FRP. In NetHack it's based on stats, luck and I think some other
factors not related to practice (I'd have to look up the code for the exact
formula). Things could be done differently, I suppose, but IMHO stats should 
probably still be the primary determinants. 

As far as reusability, I have to ask what games you are referring to? In D&D, 
every time you cast a memorized spell, the "energy of the casting wipes it 
from your memory" or some similar verbiage. It then has to be recouped in a
rested, non-combat state. Other gaming systems I've been exposed to do things 
similarly - the number of spells available in a given period of time is 
quite severely limited. Why is this?

Let's get to the bottom line here: I agree that "realistically" (if I can use
that term in reference to something as fantastical as casting magical spells)
people can usually recall things that happened in the recent past, e.g. a 
spell that was just cast. But think of the non-realism and character-imbalance
of giving magic-users an essentially infinite spell-supply: "non-realism" 
because there is no reason that magic-users wouldn't cast protective/defensive 
spells even when just walking or riding or whatever, and character-imbalance 
because for magic-users, every useful spell gained becomes useful *by an order 
of magnitude* instead of just incrementally, because it can be cast 
continually - a low- to mid-level magician who finally gets an invisibility 
spell is now "permanently" invisible in waking hours, during which he or she 
can continually cast it, and gaining a decent ranged attack such as lightning 
or fireball makes him or her essentially invulnerable to long-distance attacks, 
since the magic-user can fry the suckers before they can even get a swing. 
Compare this with fighter-types, who usually get linearly-progressing 
offensive (e.g. hit probabilities) and defensive (e.g. hit points, AC?) 
capabilities. Not that it wouldn't be possible to re-balance the classes, it's 
just a lot easier and less prone to loopholes when all character abilities go 
up linearly.

Of course, maybe you have some other limitations on spell-casting in mind,
such as limited "energy-points", or making the player hungry, both of which 
are implemented in NetHack. But these are very weak limitations, as many 
discovered in NetHack version 1, where these were the only limitations (even 
with HARD defined). Spells were *the* biggest boons in that version of the game.
